1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,869
Shine a laser through a tiny slide with the letter F on it.

2
00:00:04,939 --> 00:00:09,039
On a far wall, you don't see an F. You see streaks and fringes.

3
00:00:09,109 --> 00:00:11,735
That pattern is the Fourier transform of the F,

4
00:00:11,775 --> 00:00:14,078
computed by the light itself.

5
00:00:14,148 --> 00:00:15,970
Put a lens right behind the slide,

6
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:19,738
and the same pattern appears just one focal length away.

7
00:00:19,808 --> 00:00:22,855
Why does light compute Fourier transforms, for free?

8
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:29,077
Remember episode one: any field is a sum of plane waves,

9
00:00:29,117 --> 00:00:33,491
each heading in its own direction, sin θ = kx/k.

10
00:00:33,561 --> 00:00:37,499
Their amplitudes form the field's spectrum: its Fourier transform.

11
00:00:37,569 --> 00:00:40,192
Up close behind a slit, the waves still overlap,

12
00:00:40,232 --> 00:00:42,033
and you mostly see a shadow.

13
00:00:42,103 --> 00:00:44,632
Now step back, a thousand times farther.

14
00:00:44,702 --> 00:00:48,785
Each plane wave travels in a straight line. So far away,

15
00:00:48,825 --> 00:00:53,677
the directions come apart: direction θ lands at x = z tan θ.

16
00:00:54,347 --> 00:00:56,905
So the pattern on the wall is the spectrum itself,

17
00:00:56,945 --> 00:01:00,710
spread out in space. This is Fraunhofer diffraction.

18
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:05,384
How far is far? The pattern grows like zλ/D,

19
00:01:05,424 --> 00:01:10,388
and it must dwarf the slit width D: z ≫ D²/λ.

20
00:01:11,208 --> 00:01:14,108
But why a Fourier transform? Follow the light from

21
00:01:14,148 --> 00:01:18,064
a point x′ on the slide to a point x on the wall.

22
00:01:18,134 --> 00:01:20,667
For small angles, the path length is

23
00:01:20,707 --> 00:01:25,394
nearly a parabola: z + (x − x′)²/2z.

24
00:01:25,464 --> 00:01:28,514
The wall adds up waves from every point of the slide,

25
00:01:28,554 --> 00:01:31,049
each with phase k times that path.

26
00:01:31,119 --> 00:01:33,936
Expand the square. The x² term is a curved

27
00:01:33,976 --> 00:01:36,833
phase on the wall that brightness can't see.

28
00:01:36,903 --> 00:01:40,074
The cross term is exactly the Fourier kernel:

29
00:01:40,114 --> 00:01:45,874
position x on the wall picks out the frequency kx = k·x/z.

30
00:01:45,944 --> 00:01:48,467
The x′² term is a chirp across the slide:

31
00:01:48,507 --> 00:01:50,996
its wiggles speed up toward the edges.

32
00:01:51,036 --> 00:01:56,146
Far away it flattens to one, leaving a pure Fourier transform.

33
00:01:56,216 --> 00:01:59,350
Closer in, the chirp scrambles it. Could we cancel it?

34
00:02:00,170 --> 00:02:03,010
A lens is glass that's thicker in the middle,

35
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:05,453
so it delays the centre most.

36
00:02:05,493 --> 00:02:10,464
That's a phase e^(−ikx²/2f): an opposite chirp.

37
00:02:10,534 --> 00:02:13,455
Watch a flat wave pass through. It leaves curved,

38
00:02:13,495 --> 00:02:17,841
and converges to a point one focal length f behind the lens.

39
00:02:17,911 --> 00:02:23,026
Tilt the wave, and the focus slides sideways, to x = f tan θ.

40
00:02:23,896 --> 00:02:28,421
Three directions, three separate spots. A lens sorts light by direction.

41
00:02:28,491 --> 00:02:30,842
A slit sends out a whole spectrum of directions,

42
00:02:30,882 --> 00:02:34,997
so its spectrum appears in the focal plane, not at infinity.

43
00:02:35,067 --> 00:02:37,351
In the math, the lens's chirp exactly

44
00:02:37,391 --> 00:02:40,691
cancels the slide's chirp when z = f.

45
00:02:40,761 --> 00:02:43,627
Move the slide one focal length in front of the lens,

46
00:02:43,667 --> 00:02:48,102
and even the curved phase vanishes: an exact Fourier transform.

47
00:02:48,922 --> 00:02:52,238
Now add a second lens, one more focal length on.

48
00:02:52,278 --> 00:02:55,689
It transforms the spectrum again, and the picture comes back.

49
00:02:55,759 --> 00:02:58,680
Upside down! Two Fourier transforms don't

50
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:01,669
undo each other: they mirror x into −x.

51
00:03:01,739 --> 00:03:05,473
This is the 4f system. The middle plane holds the spectrum,

52
00:03:05,513 --> 00:03:10,416
sorted by direction, so a mask there edits it, frequency by frequency.

53
00:03:10,486 --> 00:03:14,267
A pinhole keeps only the low frequencies, and the image blurs.

54
00:03:14,337 --> 00:03:16,718
A small dot that blocks the centre keeps only

55
00:03:16,758 --> 00:03:19,949
the high frequencies, so just the edges survive.

56
00:03:20,769 --> 00:03:22,779
Now a harder case: a living cell.

57
00:03:22,819 --> 00:03:26,382
It's nearly transparent; it only delays the light a little.

58
00:03:26,452 --> 00:03:30,313
At each point, the light is a big arrow plus a tiny one at 90°,

59
00:03:30,353 --> 00:03:34,543
so its length barely changes. The camera sees nothing.

60
00:03:34,613 --> 00:03:36,718
The big arrow is light that went straight through,

61
00:03:36,758 --> 00:03:40,438
and the first lens focuses it all into the central spot.

62
00:03:40,508 --> 00:03:43,312
So delay just that spot by a quarter wave.

63
00:03:44,182 --> 00:03:46,793
Now the arrows line up, and the cell appears.

64
00:03:46,864 --> 00:03:52,339
That's Frits Zernike's phase contrast microscope, the 1953 Nobel Prize.

65
00:03:53,159 --> 00:03:56,274
On a computer, you can choose from three propagators.

66
00:03:56,344 --> 00:04:00,503
The angular spectrum method from episode one: FFT, multiply,

67
00:04:00,543 --> 00:04:06,363
inverse FFT. Exact, on the same grid as the input, and best up close.

68
00:04:06,433 --> 00:04:12,590
Single-FFT Fresnel: multiply by the chirp, then one FFT.

69
00:04:12,630 --> 00:04:15,569
The output grid stretches with distance,

70
00:04:15,609 --> 00:04:19,342
Δx′ = λz/NΔx, so it's best far away.

71
00:04:19,412 --> 00:04:23,208
And Fraunhofer: drop the chirp, and it's just one FFT.

72
00:04:23,278 --> 00:04:26,962
The catch: chirps. Once a chirp's wiggles get shorter than two samples,

73
00:04:27,002 --> 00:04:30,710
the samples trace a false, slower wave: aliasing.

74
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:34,134
The angular spectrum chirp speeds up as z grows,

75
00:04:34,174 --> 00:04:37,000
while the Fresnel chirp slows down.

76
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:40,391
They trade places at zc = NΔx²/λ:

77
00:04:40,431 --> 00:04:44,008
angular spectrum below it, Fresnel above.

78
00:04:44,828 --> 00:04:49,393
Far away, or at a lens's focus, each direction lands on its own spot,

79
00:04:49,433 --> 00:04:51,543
so light lays out its own spectrum.

80
00:04:51,613 --> 00:04:53,465
A second lens brings the picture back,

81
00:04:53,505 --> 00:04:55,678
with a mask in between to edit it.

82
00:04:55,748 --> 00:04:58,396
Where it breaks: our shortcut assumed small angles.

83
00:04:58,436 --> 00:05:02,637
For steep ones, use the exact kz from episode one.

84
00:05:02,707 --> 00:05:07,193
And a real lens is finite: it only catches directions up to some angle.

85
00:05:07,233 --> 00:05:10,100
That cutoff is why every image is blurry.

